notes[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-05T21:19:04.453Z","harness:claude-code","Design decision: compose, not refuse. list, graph and deps refuse --summary or --brief together with --full as a usage conflict, because there --full is purely a projection. For health, --full is first a check-scope flag: it overrides --check-only fast paths and the skip flags, and its help says it runs all checks including the slow integrity, drift and vectorization checks. The projection was a side effect. Fleet CI gates already call pm health --full --summary, so a refusal would break them with exit 2 on upgrade.\n\nThe flags now compose. --full decides which checks run. An explicit --summary, otherwise an explicit --brief, decides the projection. --full alone still returns complete details. --strict-exit implies the summary only when --full is absent, so --strict-exit --full keeps its documented complete details.\n\nMeasured on a one-item sandbox tracker: plain --summary already runs every check, with omitted_checks empty. --check-only --summary omits integrity, history_drift and vectorization. --check-only --full --summary now runs all ten checks and returns the summary with omitted_checks empty, and that summary is far smaller than --full (636 B against 15,704 B for the pair before the fix).\n\nThe negative control was run: reverting the three source edits fails five assertions across the new integration spec and the projection contract spec."
